ience like playing  with a train set or a dollhouse because  the game didn't explicitly tell the  player what success was it meant  they were free to define it for  themselves still unconvinced brøderbund  declined to publish SimCity right ended  up shelving the project believing that  it would never see the light of day but  a chance encounter with an entrepreneur  named Jeff Braun would solve all of his  problems Braun who had been looking to  break into the video game industry loved  the project and together they founded a  company called Maxis to publish SimCity  in 1989 after a slow start the game's  popularity began to grow by 1992 it had  sold a million copies and was being  lauded by critics and fans alike it won  countless awards and was even used in  urban planning and political science  classes realizing they'd struck a chord  with the public with Hobby style games  Maxis continued to release sim titles  throughout the 90s including sim earth  and sim ant as well as sequels to  SimCity all the while an idea had been  percolating in Wright's mind for a new  game one day while playing with his  daughter he wondered about creating a  virtual dollhouse that would appeal to  adults as much as it would children a  personal tragedy would add another  dimension to this idea in 1991 the  Wright family lost their home in a fire  as they began the process of replacing  their belongings Wright wondered why  people bought things in particular  whether they thought buying things would  fulfill their wants and needs enough to  make them happy but in order to fill a  virtual dollhouse with things he had to  design and build one first during his  research for the original SimCity he'd  made an interesting discovery 3d home  design software was flying off the  shelves realizing that people wanted to  be able to design and build their own  perfect homes but perhaps weren't able  to due to personal circumstances he  developed a prototype game where the  player could do just 