oving their skills  and gaining friends or players could  just input one of the game's infamous  cheat codes for some easy samolians but  a huge part of the game was building  relationships between Sims by  interacting with each other Sims gain or  lose relationship points Sims could  become deadly enemies and fight or form  romantic engagements Sims in a  relationship could choose to try for a  baby after 72 in-game hours the baby as  long as it hasn't been taken away by  social services will grow up into a  child but as The Sims one didn't have  any kind of genetic system the child  won't resemble either parent The Sims  also liked a real concept of time I'm  not referring to the amount of time that  players would sink into it but the fact  that there were no days of the week and  that Sims didn't age the Sims was full  of wacky motifs that were become  franchise mainstays like the ups and  downs of the goth family and the llama  which would appear in all other sim  games in some shape or form another  unusual aspect of the game was that the  Sims spoke their own unique language  Simlish Reich was afraid that having  them speak English would become extreme  be repetitive to players and also it  would be expensive to localize and  record all of that dialogue for  different countries so right reused an  idea from simcopter he enlisted voice  actors who specialized in improv to come  up with a gibberish language not only  will players be less likely to remember  the phrases and become tired of them on  repeated listenings the gibberish could  be open to interpretation further  allowing players to tell their own  stories with the game a whole Fred funda  boiler critics and fans praised the  open-endedness of the sims and it  immediately became a best-seller in just  two years it overtook Myst  to become the top-selling PC game of all  time while Wright thought that the game  would have a broad appeal he vastly  underestimated how popular it would be  with women in a 2005 interview Wright  said I thought the game would have a  broad appeal but didn't realize it would  be as big as it has become half the  players have been female they play it  for a lot longer - it is more of a hobby  a huge part of the Sims success and  longevity came from how the games  specifically geared itself towards  building and nourishing a community  according to Wright the sims could have  actually released a year early but he  had been fascinated by the community  that had been built up around Age  software's quake in particular the  amount of time and effort players were  pouring into creating their own levels  and keeping the game alive long after it  had shipped when Maxis began working on  the Sims in earnest Wright wanted to  encourage this kind of relationship  between the game and its players so max  has spent time ensuring that the  community could easily create and add  their own objects to the game they also  provided tools to help users share what  they'd created with other players  including a storytelling tool where  players could take in-game screenshots  and write a story with them this website  was known as The Sims exchange in order  to capitalize on the massive success of  the Sims and to satisfy a player base  that was crying out for more content  Maxis began to release themed expansion  packs these packs would periodically  introduced a variety of new objects  architectural styles and even new  gameplay mechanics for a lower price in  the full game they allowed Maxis to  experiment with new ideas at a  relatively low cost and kept  interest in the sims hi the first living  large released just six months after the  base game the games one neighborhood was  expanded to five new npcs who would  become series mainstays such as sunny  the tragic clown and the grim reaper  were added as well as new careers  architectural styles and twice as many  objects it also enabled two sims in a  romantic relationship to actively try  for a baby in t